To find the “standard deviation of the null hypothesis test” (i.e., the standard error of the sample mean under the null hypothesis), you divide the sample’s standard deviation by the square root of the sample size:

\[
\text{Standard Error} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}
= \frac{1.4}{\sqrt{40}}
= \frac{1.4}{6.3249}
\approx 0.221
\]

So the standard deviation (standard error) of the null hypothesis test is **≈ 0.221**.
